      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s01_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s01_c1_computer_vision_models_should_adapt",
          "text": "In computer vision, models should adapt gracefully when image resolution changes, a property called scale-equivariance.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "computer",
            "vision",
            "models",
            "should",
            "adapt",
            "gracefully",
            "when",
            "image",
            "resolution",
            "changes"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s01_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s01_c2_purdue_university_points_out_existin",
          "text": "This paper, from Purdue University, points out that existing scale-equivariant CNNs are not truly scale-equivariant, because they formulate down-scaling in the continuous domain and ignore anti-aliasing.",
          "expected_role": "figure",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "purdue",
            "university",
            "points",
            "out",
            "existing",
            "scale-equivariant",
            "cnns",
            "not",
            "truly",
            "scale-equivariant"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s01_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s01_c3_authors_instead_formulate_down_scali",
          "text": "The authors instead formulate down-scaling directly in the discrete domain with anti-aliasing, and build a new family of deep nets from Fourier layers that achieves absolute zero equivariance error, both in theory and in practice, while staying competitive on classification accuracy.",
          "expected_role": "result",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "authors",
            "instead",
            "formulate",
            "down-scaling",
            "directly",
            "discrete",
            "domain",
            "anti-aliasing",
            "build",
            "new"
          ],
          "required": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "id": "problem",
      "heading": "Problem",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s02_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s02_c1_scale_equivariance_means_when_object",
          "text": "Scale-equivariance means that when an object in an image is resized, the network's features should transform consistently, and its label should stay the same.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "scale-equivariance",
            "means",
            "when",
            "object",
            "image",
            "resized",
            "network",
            "features",
            "should",
            "transform"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s02_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s02_c2_recent_scale_equivariant_convolution",
          "text": "Recent scale-equivariant convolutional networks pursue this through weight-sharing and kernel resizing, using the same but resized kernel across scales.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "recent",
            "scale-equivariant",
            "convolutional",
            "networks",
            "pursue",
            "through",
            "weight-sharing",
            "kernel",
            "resizing",
            "same"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s02_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s02_c3_trouble_these_methods_derived_contin",
          "text": "The trouble is that these methods are derived in the continuous domain, then discretized when implemented.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "trouble",
            "these",
            "methods",
            "derived",
            "continuous",
            "domain",
            "discretized",
            "when",
            "implemented"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 4,
          "chunk_id": "s02_c04",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s02_c4_discretization_step_introduces_non_n",
          "text": "That discretization step introduces a non-negligible equivariance error, so the networks are only approximately scale-equivariant, not truly so.",
          "expected_role": "result",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "discretization",
            "step",
            "introduces",
            "non-negligible",
            "equivariance",
            "error",
            "networks",
            "only",
            "approximately",
            "scale-equivariant"
          ],
          "required": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "id": "motivation",
      "heading": "Motivation",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c1_key_insight_down_scaling_discrete_si",
          "text": "The key insight is that down-scaling a discrete signal is fundamentally a signal-processing operation.",
          "expected_role": "figure",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "key",
            "insight",
            "down-scaling",
            "discrete",
            "signal",
            "fundamentally",
            "signal-processing",
            "operation"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c2_nyquist_sampling_theorem_tells_befor",
          "text": "The Nyquist sampling theorem tells us that before subsampling, we must apply an anti-aliasing filter, otherwise high-frequency content folds down into lower frequencies, the classic aliasing artifact seen in the wagon-wheel effect.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "nyquist",
            "sampling",
            "theorem",
            "tells",
            "before",
            "subsampling",
            "must",
            "apply",
            "anti-aliasing",
            "filter"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c3_prior_scale_equivariant_networks_bec",
          "text": "Prior scale-equivariant networks, because they were formulated in the continuous domain, simply had no place for this filter.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "prior",
            "scale-equivariant",
            "networks",
            "because",
            "they",
            "formulated",
            "continuous",
            "domain",
            "simply",
            "had"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 4,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c04",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c4_authors_argue_truly_scale_equivarian",
          "text": "The authors argue that to be truly scale-equivariant, you must formulate the down-scaling directly in the discrete domain, with anti-aliasing built in from the start.",
          "expected_role": "figure",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "authors",
            "argue",
            "truly",
            "scale-equivariant",
            "you",
            "must",
            "formulate",
            "down-scaling",
            "directly",
            "discrete"
          ],
          "required": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "id": "contribution",
      "heading": "Contribution",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s04_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s04_c1_makes_three_contributions",
          "text": "The paper makes three contributions.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "makes",
            "three",
            "contributions"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s04_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s04_c2_first_formulates_down_scaling_operat",
          "text": "First, it formulates the down-scaling operation directly in the discrete domain as ideal downsampling, properly accounting for anti-aliasing.",
          "expected_role": "figure",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "first",
            "formulates",
            "down-scaling",
            "operation",
            "directly",
            "discrete",
            "domain",
            "ideal",
            "downsampling",
            "properly"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s04_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s04_c3_second_proposes_whole_family_deep",
          "text": "Second, it proposes a whole family of deep nets that are truly scale-equivariant, by rethinking every component, convolution layers, non-linearities, and pooling, and re-expressing them as Fourier layers that obey a simple frequency-dependency rule.",
          "expected_role": "guidance",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "second",
            "proposes",
            "whole",
            "family",
            "deep",
            "nets",
            "truly",
            "scale-equivariant",
            "rethinking",
            "every"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 4,
          "chunk_id": "s04_c04",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s04_c4_third_through_extensive_experiments",
          "text": "Third, through extensive experiments on MNIST-scale and STL-10, it shows the model attains an absolute zero end-to-end scale-equivariance error while remaining competitive in classification accuracy and more data-efficient in low-resource settings.",
          "expected_role": "result",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "third",
            "through",
            "extensive",
            "experiments",
            "mnist-scale",
            "stl-10",
